{
  "skillId": "find-angle-from-ratio",
  "atomType": "T",
  "theory": {
        "intro": "Given an angle, a calculator gives you the ratio. Going the other way — ratio in, angle out — needs the **inverse** trigonometric functions $\\sin^{-1}$, $\\cos^{-1}$ and $\\tan^{-1}$.",
        "facts": [
"$\\sin^{-1}(0.6314)$ is read \"sine inverse of $0.6314$\" and means **the angle whose sine ratio is $0.6314$**.",
"If $\\sin\\theta=\\dfrac{O}{H}$ then $\\theta=\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{O}{H}\\right)$, and the same pattern holds for $\\cos^{-1}$ and $\\tan^{-1}$.",
"Use an inverse function when the **angle** is the unknown; use $\\sin$, $\\cos$ or $\\tan$ when the angle is already known.",
"On a calculator, press SHIFT (or 2nd) and then the trigonometric key, with the calculator in **degree** mode.",
"Check that the calculator is in degree mode; a small answer alone does not show that the mode is wrong, because a valid angle in degrees can also be small.",
"The ratio may be a decimal ($0.7431$) or a fraction ($\\dfrac{8}{17}$); enter it exactly as given and round only at the end.",
"The question states the rounding: \"to the nearest degree\" gives a whole number of degrees, \"to $4$ decimal places\" keeps four decimals of a degree."
],
        "steps": [
          "Write the angle in inverse form",
          "Evaluate with a calculator in degree mode",
          "Round as the question asks"
        ]
      },
